# A version named relative to the writer's present (`in older
# versions`, `newer kernels`, `a recent version of client-go`) leaves
# the reader to guess where the line falls, and the line moves with
# every release. The fix names the version (`before v1.24`, `kernels
# 5.8 and later`). The dated-claim rule catches a claim dated to the
# present, and the roadmap rule catches `in a future release`.
#
# `older versions` and its kin count only after `in` or before `of`,
# and `recent` and `newer` count only after `a` or `the`, because a
# bare `older versions` often means any earlier version. `modern`,
# `newer`, `older`, and `recent` count before a noun for hardware or
# platforms (`modern CPUs`, `older kernels`).
#
# It hit 16 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 13 of 16 left the
# version to the reader. The misses were a generic `earlier versions of
# an API`, `older versions of Kubernetes` with a clause that defines
# them, and `modern CPUs`, which has no version to name.
expr when [older, earlier, newer, recent, prior, previous]
expr main [(in @when [versions, releases]), ([a, the] [recent, newer] [version, release]), ([newer, older, recent, modern] [kernels, browsers, CPUs, processors, distributions, compilers]), ([older, newer, earlier, recent] versions of)]
let message "Relative version. Name the version, such as `before v1.24` or `5.8 and later`."
let description "Flags `in older versions`, `newer kernels`, and similar phrases that name a version relative to the present."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Before v1.24, the flag defaulted to false."
allows "Kernels 5.8 and later support the option."
allows "Roll back to the previous version of the Deployment."
